Persistent state: a row may carry a persist entry beside its main. Where main runs in a
forked child with a fresh state every call, persist runs in this process against one
state kept for its life, so what one $(name.persistent) call leaves behind the next
finds. The result is what the entry writes to the stream it is given, trimmed the way
every builtin's output is, and its return is the status. A zygote's fork carries the
state, so each request starts from what the parse left. Lua takes the entry here; the
row field is null for an engine without one, and no builtin is registered for it.

